---
![плавание брассом](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6e3f564c-0667-49b2-b2f3-3eba0da4794b)

{{% steps %}}

### Step 1
Starting position: body fully stretched out, arms straight out in front of you, hands close together.

### Step 2
Sweep your hands outward slightly wider than shoulder-width.

### Step 3
Bend your elbows and pull water back towards you until your hands reach about shoulder level.

### Step 4
The pulling motion naturally lifts your chest and head up, bringing your face out of the water.

### Step 5
At the end of the pull, bring your hands together in front of your chest, palms facing your chest.

### Step 6
As your head clears the water, take a quick, sharp breath in through your mouth.

### Step 7
Bend your knees to prepare for the kick (keep your knees relatively close together, do not spread them too wide).

### Step 8
Quickly shoot your arms forward to straighten them out.

### Step 9
At the same time, point your feet outward and deliver a powerful kick back with your legs.

### Step 10
Lower your face back into the water as your arms extend.

### Step 11
Glide forward in a straight, streamlined line. Once the glide slows down, repeat the cycle.

### Step 12
Breathing: Inhale quickly through your mouth as your head rises above the water.

### Step 13
Exhale steadily through your nose (and mouth if needed) the entire time your face is underwater. Do not hold your breath—start exhaling the moment your face hits the water.

{{% /steps %}}

{{< callout type="info" >}}
⠀• Keep your head neutral with your neck straight by looking down at the water during both breathing and gliding.
⠀• One powerful, efficient stroke will push you much farther than several short, rushed strokes.
⠀• Never skip the glide—it is the fastest and easiest phase of the stroke.
⠀• Keep your kick compact (about shoulder-width); kicking too wide creates extra drag and slows you down.
⠀• Keep the entire cycle fluid: pull, breathe, kick, and glide in one continuous rhythm.
⠀• Flex your ankles outwards during the kick to push more water.
⠀• Keep your head low and keep your fingers close together so water doesn't slip through your hands.
⠀• Do not overtense your leg muscles to avoid painful cramps.
⠀• In shallow water, be mindful of the bottom so you don't hit the pool floor with your feet or knees.
{{< /callout >}}
